vms-7b96: defer the DST slurp at object-scan time (vms-alpha BFD read)

The alpha-dec-vms cross toolchain emits a VMS Debug Symbol Table (DST) as an
EDBG/ETBT record for -gvms objects (musl's libc.a is built -gvms).  On read,
alpha_vms_object_p slurps every record at bfd_check_format time; for EDBG/ETBT
that replays the DST -- an ETIR-form store stream -- into a freshly made,
size-0 $DST$ section.  Two things in the reader's store machinery then reject
the whole object as bfd_error_wrong_format -> "file format not recognized":

  1. image_write bounds-checks every store against the current section size and
     cannot grow it, so the first STO_IMM overruns the size-0 $DST$; and
  2. even sized, the traceback DST's section-relative STO_LW store takes the
     relocation branch into alpha_vms_add_lw_reloc, a binutils-2.43 stub that
     unconditionally returns false -- and the check-format replay runs with
     info == NULL (there is no link), so there is no reloc context anyway.

The net effect is that nm/objdump/addr2line cannot read ANY -gvms object
(every member of the musl alpha-dec-vms libc.a).

The object-scan pass is not meant to perform image writes at all: it runs
before sections are sized, which is exactly why the neighbouring ETIR case is
already a no-op (`ok = true; /* _bfd_vms_slurp_etir (abfd); */`).  This makes
EDBG/ETBT consistent with it and defers the DST the same way.  The symbols
nm/objdump -t report come from the EGSD record read earlier in the same scan,
so -gvms objects now load and are symbol-inspectable.  This cannot regress a
previously-readable object: it only removes a failing operation.  DST line-info
decoding (replaying the store stream with a link context) is a separate
enhancement with no current consumer -- the port's real symbol proof is
LINK.EXE consuming these members to emit DECC$SHR.

Upstream binutils 2.43 behaviour (the OVMX 0001-0004 patches touch only the
emit side); it never bites in normal VMS use because GNU nm is not run on DST
objects there.  Clean-room (Rule 8): derived from the public binutils source +
the record framing observed empirically; no VSI/HPE material.

--- a/bfd/vms-alpha.c	2026-08-26 21:06:04.282802966 +0000
+++ b/bfd/vms-alpha.c	2026-08-26 21:06:10.893714727 +0000
@@ -2723,10 +2723,22 @@
 	  ok = true; /* _bfd_vms_slurp_etir (abfd); */
 	  break;
 	case EOBJ__C_EDBG:
-	  ok = _bfd_vms_slurp_edbg (abfd);
-	  break;
 	case EOBJ__C_ETBT:
-	  ok = _bfd_vms_slurp_etbt (abfd);
+	  /* vms-7b96: defer DST replay, exactly as ETIR above is deferred.  The
+	     object-scan pass runs before any section is sized; slurping the DST
+	     here (an ETIR-form store stream carried by EDBG/ETBT) replays it into
+	     a size-0 $DST$ section, where the store machinery -- the image_write
+	     bounds check, then the STO_LW/add_lw_reloc reloc path (a stub that
+	     returns false when info == NULL, as it is at check-format time) --
+	     makes _bfd_vms_slurp_etir fail and the whole object is rejected as
+	     bfd_error_wrong_format ("file format not recognized").  That left
+	     nm/objdump unable to read any -gvms object (every member of the musl
+	     alpha-dec-vms libc.a).  The symbols nm/objdump -t report come from the
+	     EGSD record read earlier, so deferring the DST lets these objects load
+	     and be symbol-inspected.  Decoding the DST (line info) needs the replay
+	     run with a link context and is a separate enhancement with no current
+	     consumer.  */
+	  ok = true;
 	  break;
 	default:
 	  ok = false;
